F24 L33 TestIdentityEpochChange/F: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. Admit via F. → Change only the original account's Epoch to 8. → Invoke the callback while Epoch remains 8. => observed-in-code — Original Requests remain 1 and original Successes increase to 1.
F25 L10 TestIdentityCompletionBeforeBoundary/A: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. A replacement account starts with zero counters. Admit via A. → Invoke the callback and inspect the original counters. → Change the original epoch to 8, set Port.Target to nil, then bind the replacement. Make no later callback invocation. => observed-in-code — The original already has Requests=1 and Successes=1 before the changes and retains both afterward. The replacement remains at zero.
F26 L15 TestIdentityCompletionBeforeBoundary/B: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. A replacement account starts with zero counters. Admit via B. → Invoke the callback and inspect the original counters. → Change the original epoch to 8, set Port.Target to nil, then bind the replacement. Make no later callback invocation. => observed-in-code — The original already has Requests=1 and Successes=1 before the changes and retains both afterward. The replacement remains at zero.
F27 L20 TestIdentityCompletionBeforeBoundary/C: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. A replacement account starts with zero counters. Admit via C. → Invoke the callback and inspect the original counters. → Change the original epoch to 8, set Port.Target to nil, then bind the replacement. Make no later callback invocation. => observed-in-code — The original already has Requests=1 and Successes=1 before the changes and retains both afterward. The replacement remains at zero.
F28 L24 TestIdentityCompletionBeforeBoundary/D: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. A replacement account starts with zero counters. Admit via D. → Invoke the callback and inspect the original counters. → Change the original epoch to 8, delete the admitted key, then bind the replacement. Make no later callback invocation. => observed-in-code — The original already has Requests=1 and Successes=1 before the changes and retains both afterward. The replacement remains at zero.
F29 L28 TestIdentityCompletionBeforeBoundary/E: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. A replacement account starts with zero counters. Admit via E. → Invoke the callback and inspect the original counters. → Change the original epoch to 8, set Port.Target to nil, then bind the replacement. Make no later callback invocation. => observed-in-code — The original already has Requests=1 and Successes=1 before the changes and retains both afterward. The replacement remains at zero.
F30 L33 TestIdentityCompletionBeforeBoundary/F: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. A replacement account starts with zero counters. Admit via F. → Invoke the callback and inspect the original counters. → Change the original epoch to 8, set Port.Target to nil, then bind the replacement. Make no later callback invocation. => observed-in-code — The original already has Requests=1 and Successes=1 before the changes and retains both afterward. The replacement remains at zero.
F31 L10 TestIdentityDetachedPort/A: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. Admit via A. → Set Port.Target to nil. → Invoke and recover the callback panic. → Restore the original and invoke the same callback again. => observed-in-code — The first invocation panics while reading the nil target's Epoch and does not credit success. Restoring the binding alone does not credit success. The later invocation credits the original once.
F32 L15 TestIdentityDetachedPort/B: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. Admit via B. → Set Port.Target to nil. → Invoke the callback. → Restore the original and invoke the same callback again. => observed-in-code — The detached callback credits the original once. The later invocation credits the original again.
F33 L20 TestIdentityDetachedPort/C: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. Admit via C. → Set Port.Target to nil. → Invoke the callback. → Restore the original and invoke the same callback again. => observed-in-code — The first invocation returns normally and the original remains at Requests=1, Successes=0. Restoring its binding alone leaves the counters unchanged. The later invocation credits the original once.
F34 L28 TestIdentityDetachedPort/E: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. Admit via E. → Set Port.Target to nil. → Invoke the callback. → Restore the original and invoke the same callback again. => observed-in-code — The detached callback credits the original once. The later invocation credits the original again.
F35 L33 TestIdentityDetachedPort/F: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. Admit via F. → Set Port.Target to nil. → Invoke the callback. → Restore the original and invoke the same callback again. => observed-in-code — The detached callback credits the original once. The later invocation credits the original again.
F36 L24 TestIdentityRegistryAbsent/no_later_input: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. The key maps to the original. Admit via D. → Delete the key. → Invoke while the entry is unavailable. → Leave the entry unavailable and make no later invocation. => observed-in-code — The callback returns normally and the original remains at Requests=1, Successes=0.
F37 L24 TestIdentityRegistryAbsent/later_matching_key: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. The key maps to the original. Admit via D. → Delete the key. → Invoke while the entry is unavailable. → Bind a replacement at the same key, creating a map if needed. Inspect counters before invoking. → Invoke the same callback again. => observed-in-code — The unavailable invocation and binding alone leave Successes unchanged. The later invocation credits the replacement once, leaving the original request in place.
F38 L24 TestIdentityRegistryNilEntry/no_later_input: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. The key maps to the original. Admit via D. → Store nil under the key. → Invoke while the entry is unavailable. → Leave the entry unavailable and make no later invocation. => observed-in-code — The callback returns normally and the original remains at Requests=1, Successes=0.
F39 L24 TestIdentityRegistryNilEntry/later_matching_key: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. The key maps to the original. Admit via D. → Store nil under the key. → Invoke while the entry is unavailable. → Bind a replacement at the same key, creating a map if needed. Inspect counters before invoking. → Invoke the same callback again. => observed-in-code — The unavailable invocation and binding alone leave Successes unchanged. The later invocation credits the replacement once, leaving the original request in place.
F40 L24 TestIdentityRegistryNilMap/no_later_input: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. The key maps to the original. Admit via D. → Set Registry.Entries to nil. → Invoke while the entry is unavailable. → Leave the entry unavailable and make no later invocation. => observed-in-code — The callback returns normally and the original remains at Requests=1, Successes=0.
F41 L24 TestIdentityRegistryNilMap/later_matching_key: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. The key maps to the original. Admit via D. → Set Registry.Entries to nil. → Invoke while the entry is unavailable. → Bind a replacement at the same key, creating a map if needed. Inspect counters before invoking. → Invoke the same callback again. => observed-in-code — The unavailable invocation and binding alone leave Successes unchanged. The later invocation credits the replacement once, leaving the original request in place.
F42 L24 TestIdentityRegistryMapReplacement: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. A replacement account has Epoch=8. Admit via D. → Assign a fresh map containing the same key with the replacement and a distinct other key. → Invoke the callback. => observed-in-code — The replacement receives one success. The old map's account retains its request and has no success. The other key's account remains untouched.
F43 L10 TestIdentityEpochRestoration/A: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. Admit via A. → Change the original epoch to 8. → Invoke and inspect counters. → Restore epoch 7 and inspect counters again. → Invoke the same callback again. => observed-in-code — The first call and the epoch restoration alone leave original Successes at zero. The later matching invocation credits one success.
F44 L20 TestIdentityEpochRestoration/C: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. Admit via C. → Change the original epoch to 8. → Invoke and inspect counters. → Restore epoch 7 and inspect counters again. → Invoke the same callback again. => observed-in-code — The first call and the epoch restoration alone leave original Successes at zero. The later matching invocation credits one success.
F45 L10 TestIdentityTargetRestoration/A: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. A replacement has Epoch=7. Admit via A. → Bind the replacement. → Invoke and inspect both accounts. → Restore the original and invoke the same callback again. => observed-in-code — The first invocation credits the replacement once. The later invocation credits the original once.
F46 L20 TestIdentityTargetRestoration/C: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. A replacement has Epoch=7. Admit via C. → Bind the replacement. → Invoke and inspect both accounts. → Restore the original and invoke the same callback again. => observed-in-code — The first invocation credits neither account. The later invocation credits the original once.
F47 L10 TestIdentityAbandonedAndRepeated/A: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. Admit two operations via A through the same binding. → Discard one returned callback and retain the other. Inspect counters with no completion yet. → Invoke the retained callback twice under the original binding, with a counter check after each call. → Make no invocation of the discarded callback. => observed-in-code — Two requests are recorded and zero successes exist before any callback. The retained callback credits one success on each invocation. There is no additional credit or rollback attributable to the discarded callback in this sequence.
F48 L15 TestIdentityAbandonedAndRepeated/B: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. Admit two operations via B through the same binding. → Discard one returned callback and retain the other. Inspect counters with no completion yet. → Invoke the retained callback twice under the original binding, with a counter check after each call. → Make no invocation of the discarded callback. => observed-in-code — Two requests are recorded and zero successes exist before any callback. The retained callback credits one success on each invocation. There is no additional credit or rollback attributable to the discarded callback in this sequence.
F49 L20 TestIdentityAbandonedAndRepeated/C: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. Admit two operations via C through the same binding. → Discard one returned callback and retain the other. Inspect counters with no completion yet. → Invoke the retained callback twice under the original binding, with a counter check after each call. → Make no invocation of the discarded callback. => observed-in-code — Two requests are recorded and zero successes exist before any callback. The retained callback credits one success on each invocation. There is no additional credit or rollback attributable to the discarded callback in this sequence.
F50 L24 TestIdentityAbandonedAndRepeated/D: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. Admit two operations via D through the same binding. → Discard one returned callback and retain the other. Inspect counters with no completion yet. → Invoke the retained callback twice under the original binding, with a counter check after each call. → Make no invocation of the discarded callback. => observed-in-code — Two requests are recorded and zero successes exist before any callback. The retained callback credits one success on each invocation. There is no additional credit or rollback attributable to the discarded callback in this sequence.
F51 L28 TestIdentityAbandonedAndRepeated/E: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. Admit two operations via E through the same binding. → Discard one returned callback and retain the other. Inspect counters with no completion yet. → Invoke the retained callback twice under the original binding, with a counter check after each call. → Make no invocation of the discarded callback. => observed-in-code — Two requests are recorded and zero successes exist before any callback. The retained callback credits one success on each invocation. There is no additional credit or rollback attributable to the discarded callback in this sequence.
F52 L33 TestIdentityAbandonedAndRepeated/F: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. Admit two operations via F through the same binding. → Discard one returned callback and retain the other. Inspect counters with no completion yet. → Invoke the retained callback twice under the original binding, with a counter check after each call. → Make no invocation of the discarded callback. => observed-in-code — Two requests are recorded and zero successes exist before any callback. The retained callback credits one success on each invocation. There is no additional credit or rollback attributable to the discarded callback in this sequence.
F53 L10 TestIdentityOverlappingAdmissions/A: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. A replacement account also has Epoch=7. Admit via A on the original. → Rebind to the replacement and admit another operation through the same Port or Registry. → Invoke the newer callback first. → Invoke the older callback last. => observed-in-code — Each account has one request. The newer callback credits the replacement once. The older callback credits the replacement again.
F54 L15 TestIdentityOverlappingAdmissions/B: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. A replacement account also has Epoch=7. Admit via B on the original. → Rebind to the replacement and admit another operation through the same Port or Registry. → Invoke the newer callback first. → Invoke the older callback last. => observed-in-code — Each account has one request. The newer callback credits the replacement once. The older callback credits the original once.
F55 L20 TestIdentityOverlappingAdmissions/C: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. A replacement account also has Epoch=7. Admit via C on the original. → Rebind to the replacement and admit another operation through the same Port or Registry. → Invoke the newer callback first. → Invoke the older callback last. => observed-in-code — Each account has one request. The newer callback credits the replacement once. The older callback adds no credit.
F56 L24 TestIdentityOverlappingAdmissions/D: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. A replacement account also has Epoch=7. Admit via D on the original. → Rebind to the replacement and admit another operation through the same Port or Registry. → Invoke the newer callback first. → Invoke the older callback last. => observed-in-code — Each account has one request. The newer callback credits the replacement once. The older callback credits the replacement again.
