F59 L8 TestIdentityInvalidAdmission/A: A non-nil Port has a nil Target. Call A for each applicable invalid input and recover. => observed-in-code — Admission panics at the request increment and does not return a callback.
F60 L14 TestIdentityInvalidAdmission/B: A non-nil Port has a nil Target. Call B for each applicable invalid input and recover. => observed-in-code — Admission panics at the request increment and does not return a callback.
F61 L18 TestIdentityInvalidAdmission/C: A non-nil Port has a nil Target. Call C for each applicable invalid input and recover. => observed-in-code — Admission panics while evaluating p.Target.Epoch, before the Requests increment. No callback is returned.
F62 L23 TestIdentityInvalidAdmission/D: The Registry has an absent key, a nil-valued key, or a nil map. Call D for each applicable invalid input and recover. => observed-in-code — A missing key, a nil entry, and a nil map each yield a nil account at the lookup. The unguarded Requests dereference panics and no callback is returned.
F63 L27 TestIdentityInvalidAdmission/E: A non-nil Port has a nil Target. Call E for each applicable invalid input and recover. => observed-in-code — Admission panics at the request increment and does not return a callback.
F64 L32 TestIdentityInvalidAdmission/F: A non-nil Port has a nil Target. Call F for each applicable invalid input and recover. => observed-in-code — Admission panics at the request increment and does not return a callback.
F65 L28 TestIdentityValueCopy: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. A replacement has Epoch=8. Copy the Port value into another caller variable. → Admit via E using the first Port. → Set the first Port's Target to the replacement and the other Port's Target to nil. → Invoke the callback. => observed-in-code — The original shared Account gets one request and one success. The replacement remains untouched. The copied receiver retains the admitted account pointer.
F66 L15 TestIdentityCapturedObjectAfterReset: The original account has Requests=0, Successes=0, and Epoch=7. Admit via B. → Reset original Requests and Successes to zero, then set Successes to 10. → Invoke the callback. => observed-in-code — The admitted account ends at Requests=0 and Successes=11. The callback increments the existing field on the captured object.
F67 L10 TestIdentityCallerDeparture/A: The original and a distinct replacement both have Epoch=7 and zero counters. Admit via A on the original and retain the callback on the delivery side after the initiating call returns. → Rebind the same Port or Registry key to the replacement while the initiating side no longer waits. → Compare two schedules. End without any invocation, or invoke the retained callback once after the rebind. => observed-in-code — Without an invocation the original remains at Requests=1 and Successes=0, and the replacement counters stay zero. With a later invocation, the replacement gains one success while the original retains the request and has zero successes.
F68 L15 TestIdentityCallerDeparture/B: The original and a distinct replacement both have Epoch=7 and zero counters. Admit via B on the original and retain the callback on the delivery side after the initiating call returns. → Rebind the same Port or Registry key to the replacement while the initiating side no longer waits. → Compare two schedules. End without any invocation, or invoke the retained callback once after the rebind. => observed-in-code — Without an invocation the original remains at Requests=1 and Successes=0, and the replacement counters stay zero. With a later invocation, the original gains one success, retaining its request, and both replacement counters stay zero.
F69 L20 TestIdentityCallerDeparture/C: The original and a distinct replacement both have Epoch=7 and zero counters. Admit via C on the original and retain the callback on the delivery side after the initiating call returns. → Rebind the same Port or Registry key to the replacement while the initiating side no longer waits. → Compare two schedules. End without any invocation, or invoke the retained callback once after the rebind. => observed-in-code — Without an invocation the original remains at Requests=1 and Successes=0, and the replacement counters stay zero. With a later invocation, neither account gains a success and the original retains the request.
F70 L24 TestIdentityCallerDeparture/D: The original and a distinct replacement both have Epoch=7 and zero counters. Admit via D on the original and retain the callback on the delivery side after the initiating call returns. → Rebind the same Port or Registry key to the replacement while the initiating side no longer waits. → Compare two schedules. End without any invocation, or invoke the retained callback once after the rebind. => observed-in-code — Without an invocation the original remains at Requests=1 and Successes=0, and the replacement counters stay zero. With a later invocation, the replacement gains one success while the original retains the request and has zero successes.
F71 L28 TestIdentityCallerDeparture/E: The original and a distinct replacement both have Epoch=7 and zero counters. Admit via E on the original and retain the callback on the delivery side after the initiating call returns. → Rebind the same Port or Registry key to the replacement while the initiating side no longer waits. → Compare two schedules. End without any invocation, or invoke the retained callback once after the rebind. => observed-in-code — Without an invocation the original remains at Requests=1 and Successes=0, and the replacement counters stay zero. With a later invocation, the original gains one success, retaining its request, and both replacement counters stay zero.
F72 L33 TestIdentityCallerDeparture/F: The original and a distinct replacement both have Epoch=7 and zero counters. Admit via F on the original and retain the callback on the delivery side after the initiating call returns. → Rebind the same Port or Registry key to the replacement while the initiating side no longer waits. → Compare two schedules. End without any invocation, or invoke the retained callback once after the rebind. => observed-in-code — Without an invocation the original remains at Requests=1 and Successes=0, and the replacement counters stay zero. With a later invocation, the original gains one success, retaining its request, and both replacement counters stay zero.
F73 L10 TestIdentityMatchingAfterMismatch/A: The original account has Epoch=7 and a different account has Epoch=8. Admit via A on the original. → Bind the replacement and invoke the callback. → Set the replacement's Epoch to 7 and inspect counters without calling again. → Invoke the same callback again. => observed-in-code — The first invocation and epoch edit leave both Successes counters unchanged. The later invocation credits the replacement once. The original retains the single request.
F74 L20 TestIdentityMatchingAfterMismatch/C: The original and a different account both start at Epoch=7. Admit via C on the original. → Change the original Epoch to 8 and bind the replacement. → Invoke with just the replacement's epoch matching, then restore only the original pointer and invoke again. → Restore the original epoch to 7. Inspect the counters, then invoke again. => observed-in-code — Neither a different matching-epoch account nor the original account at a different epoch is credited. The epoch restoration alone also leaves successes unchanged. Once both admission conditions hold, invoking the same callback credits the original once.
TOP LEVEL ['findings']
